.nh
.TH "TUSK" "1" "Jul 2026" "Tusk" "Tusk Manual"

.SH NAME
tusk-query - Run a structural, semantic, or hybrid query against the index


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtusk query  [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Run a query against the index.

.PP
Three modes, all driven by the same command:
.IP \(bu 2
Structural (default): the filter argument is a property and
edge-traversal expression. Property predicates use comparison
operators — = and : (equal), != (not equal), < <= > >= (ordering) —
written directly after the key (e.g. status=open, priority>=2);
ranges use key=lo..hi. != and NOT also match a node without the
property; key=* means it has a value and key!=* that it is missing.
A list property takes ~= for one item (labels~=bug) and key=[a, b]
for every listed item, in any order. Ordering and range operators
compare by the property's declared type: int numerically,
date/datetime chronologically, enum by declared order (a value name
or a 0-based index). A date or datetime also takes a relative date:
today, today-90d or today+2w (units h, d, w, m for months, y), and
now-48h for a datetime. path and id also take glob patterns with = and !=: * stays
inside one folder, ? is one character, and a ** segment crosses
folders (e.g. path=docs/product/*, path=docs/\fB, id=\fP/index); quote
the value to match a literal *. Edge traversal uses edge-type-> (outgoing) or edge-type<-
(incoming) and may chain multi-hop. The one term after the arrow (a
predicate, NOT term, or parenthesized group) constrains the linked
node, e.g. blocks-> (status=open OR status=wip). ->+ repeats the hop
to any depth and ->{2}, ->{1,3} or ->{2,} a number of times, stopping
on cycles (e.g. depends-on->+ id=payments). An edge type's inverse
label walks it backwards (depended-on-by-> is depends-on<-).
Traversal shortcuts: tree=id, parent=id, root=id, each optionally
qualified by a hierarchy alias (e.g. tree:wbs=id) set via hierarchy
on an edge type in tusk.toml; parent-is-source = true there means the
parent names its children, and max-depth caps tree= and root=. Recency shortcut: modified-since: a duration or ISO date
(e.g. modified-since:7d, modified-since:2026-05-23). Path refs:
names-path= finds the pages that name a workspace path (in
inline code, a link, or a paths-only frontmatter value), or a
directory above it; names-path= matches the named paths
themselves (e.g. names-path=server/**). It needs an edge type with
paths = true in tusk.toml; names-path:= matches
that edge type's refs only. Combine with AND, OR, NOT, and
parens. (Both : and = bind property comparisons; pick whichever
reads better.)
.IP \(bu 2
Semantic (--semantic STRING): nearest-neighbor search over
Ollama embeddings. The positional filter still applies as a
pre-filter; pass a permissive filter like 'type=note' to search
a whole type, or '' to search everything.
.IP \(bu 2
Hybrid: structural filter narrows the candidate set, then
--semantic ranks it by cosine similarity.

.PP
Use --sort to order by one or more keys (prefix +/-), --take N to limit
results, --skip M to paginate. Use --include to expand each row with
body, edges, properties, units or paths (comma-separated; for semantic
results body gives every row's best unit its text). Use --fields to project
the rendered shape.
Use --format to pick compact or JSON output: with no --include/--fields
the default is the tab-aligned table, and once a shape flag is set it is
compact at a TTY and JSON when piped. --json (or --format json) forces
JSON regardless.

.PP
Matched units: semantic rows always carry matched_units, the passages that
matched. Each scored passage folds into its innermost section, so one finding
is one row: the section's id, heading, and line range, with the passage's
score and snippet. A passage before the first heading is its own row.
In JSON a semantic unit carries id, heading, lines, score and snippet; the
row's own snippet appears only on a row with no units, and scores are rounded
to three decimals. The top row's best unit carries text, its section's own
markdown read from the file (no heading line or subsections; a window of
whole paragraphs and items around the match past 2 KB), and text_lines, the
lines it covers, in place of the snippet. --include body gives every row's
best unit its text. A file edited since it was indexed gets no text.
--include units lists every file's sub-unit outline on structural queries.
Every unit carries lines start, end
\[la]1\-based, inclusive, counted from the
top of the file; absent for HTML\[ra], numbered under [workspace] line-numbering.
--max-units N keeps the first N units per file (best first on semantic rows,
document order on the outline); units_total reports how many there were.

.PP
Path refs: --include paths lists the workspace paths each page names in
inline code, links and images, or a paths-only frontmatter value, one entry
per path and edge type, with every line it is named on, the innermost
section holding that line, and exists, a live check of the disk. Only paths
whose first segment exists at the workspace root count. Under a names-path
filter the list keeps the refs the filter matched.

.PP
Sub-unit addresses: a sub-unit's id appends a structural address to the file
id, e.g. notes/doc#S1.2P3 (paragraph 3 of section 1.2) or notes/doc#S1.1T1R0C0
(a table cell). Addresses stay stable under in-place edits and shift only when
the document is restructured.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB--explain\fP[=false]
	include a per-row score trace: cosine and final, plus lexical, title, graph/distance and weight when the ranking computed them

.PP
\fB--fields\fP=[]
	project rendered rows to these fields (comma-separated)

.PP
\fB--format\fP=""
	output format: compact|json (default: tab-aligned table; with --include/--fields, compact for TTY and json when piped)

.PP
\fB--graph-edges\fP=[]
	comma-separated edge-type names used by the graph expander; omit to inherit manifest

.PP
\fB--graph-expand\fP[=false]
	enable graph-expanded retrieval for this call (overrides [query.graph-expansion] enabled=false)

.PP
\fB--graph-weight\fP=inherit
	per-hop weight applied to expanded candidates in [0,1]; omit to inherit manifest

.PP
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for query

.PP
\fB--hops\fP=0
	graph-expansion BFS depth (1 or 2; omit to inherit manifest)

.PP
\fB--include\fP=[]
	expand rows: body|edges|properties|units|paths (comma-separated; units lists each file's sub-units, paths the workspace paths each page names)

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	emit structured JSON (sugar for --format json)

.PP
\fB--max-units\fP=0
	keep at most N matched units per file (0 = all); units_total reports the count before the cut

.PP
\fB--min-score\fP=0
	drop semantic results below this similarity score (default 0 = no filter; MCP tusk_query defaults to 0.5). When graph expansion is active, this filters the blended final score, not the bare cosine.

.PP
\fB--no-graph-expand\fP[=false]
	disable graph-expanded retrieval for this call (beats [query.graph-expansion] enabled=true)

.PP
\fB--semantic\fP=""
	rank results by cosine similarity to this query string (requires [embeddings] in tusk.toml)

.PP
\fB--skip\fP=0
	skip the first M rows (requires --take)

.PP
\fB--sort\fP=""
	sort spec, e.g., +priority,-due,+modified

.PP
\fB--take\fP=0
	limit results to N rows


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB-v\fP, \fB--verbose\fP[=false]
	emit debug-level logs to stderr

.PP
\fB-C\fP, \fB--workspace\fP=""
	run as if started in \fBdir\fR: find tusk.toml from there instead of the working directory (default $TUSK_WORKSPACE)


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  # Structural: all priority-1 tickets touched in the last week
  tusk query 'type=ticket AND priority=1 AND modified-since:7d'

  # Tickets with no owner, or labelled both bug and urgent
  tusk query 'type=ticket AND owner!=*'
  tusk query 'type=ticket AND labels=[bug, urgent]'

  # Services not reviewed in the last 90 days (relative to today)
  tusk query 'type=service AND reviewed<today-90d'

  # Expand bodies and edges in one round-trip
  tusk query 'type=ticket' --include body,edges

  # Which pages describe this file (or a directory above it), and where
  tusk query 'names-path=server/ledger/core/service.go' --include paths

  # The same, through one paths edge type only
  tusk query 'names-path:describes=server/ledger/core/service.go'

  # Pure semantic over all notes
  tusk query 'type=note' --semantic 'cache invalidation strategies'

  # Hybrid: filter to design notes, then rank by similarity
  tusk query 'type=note AND kind=design' --semantic 'sqlite write contention'

  # Pipe top match into "node get"
  tusk query 'type=note' --semantic 'auth flow' --json --take 1 \\
    | jq -r '.[0].id' \\
    | xargs tusk node get
.EE


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBtusk(1)\fP
